course: books
lesson: 83
title: Just So Stories, The Beginning of the Armadillos, part 2: swimming lessons, curling lessons, and a new name
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Hedgehog and Tortoise know Jaguar will never forget his rhyme, so they decide to swap skills. Tortoise holds up Hedgehog's chin while he learns to swim; Hedgehog unlaces Tortoise's back-plates so he can bend and curl a tiny bit. All night they cheer each other on with «Excellent!» and «Won't Painted Jaguar be surprised!»
explain: By morning both have changed. Tortoise's plates now overlap, and Hedgehog's prickles have melted together into scales, so he looks more like a pine-cone than a prickly chestnut-case. Both can swim and both can curl.
explain: They go and find Jaguar, who is so astonished that he falls backwards over his tail three times. They say his rhyme back to him with the names swapped and roll round and round him until his eyes spin like cart-wheels.
explain: Jaguar fetches his Mother. She says a Hedgehog is a Hedgehog and a Tortoise is a Tortoise, but when he says this is a bit of both, she says she would call it «Armadillo» till she found the real name, and leave it alone. And so, says Kipling, they have been called Armadillos ever since.
explain: The story ends with a song: Kipling has never sailed the Amazon or seen a jaguar or an armadillo, but he would love to «roll down to Rio» one day before he is old, on one of the big steamers that sail «weekly from Southampton» (an English port).
fact: Jaguar sits on the bank sucking prickles from his paws and saying his rhyme; Hedgehog says he will not forget it «this month of Sundays», which means for a very long time.
fact: Hedgehog says «Hold up my chin, Slow-and-Solid. I'm going to try to learn to swim. It may be useful.»
fact: Hedgehog unlaces Tortoise's back-plates, «two holes more» later, so Tortoise can bend; when the curling makes Tortoise go black in the face, Hedgehog tells him to stop for now. Tortoise practises putting his hind legs round his ears, which he calls «so peculiarly comfortable».
fact: Hedgehog learns the side-stroke and the long dive; Tortoise says that with practice he will be «a regular whale».
fact: Each friend calls the other's skill «so easy» and keeps saying «Won't Painted Jaguar be surprised!»; they help each other, which is the heart of the story.
fact: In the morning Tortoise's plates are «all overlapping now, instead of lying side by side», and Hedgehog's prickles are «melting into one another», like a pinecone, «less like a chestnut-burr» (the prickly case of a chestnut).
fact: Hedgehog says «scales are a tremendous improvement on prickles». Jaguar «fell three times backward over his own painted tail».
fact: The friends tease Jaguar with his rhyme swapped round: «Can't curl, but can swim, Stickly-Prickly, that's him!»
fact: Jaguar tells his Mother that the two «have gone shares in their prickles», because both are scaly all over.
fact: Mother Jaguar: «Everything has its proper name. I should call it "Armadillo" till I found out the real one. And I should leave it alone.»
fact: Real life: armadillos are mammals, with hair on their bellies and babies that drink milk; they are not a mix of hedgehog and tortoise. Their armour is made of small bony plates covered with tough skin.
fact: Real life: the name «armadillo» is Spanish for «little armoured one», given by Spanish speakers in the Americas, not by a jaguar. Armadillos live from the southern USA down to Argentina.
fact: Real life: only the three-banded armadillos can roll into a full ball; the nine-banded armadillo can swim, hold its breath for about six minutes, and even walk along the bottom of a stream.
fact: Real life: animals do not change their bodies by practising for one night; living things change very slowly, over thousands of generations. Kipling's tale, like the Rhinoceros's folds and the Kangaroo's legs, is a made-up «just so» reason.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «no cap» = honestly, no lie; «ate» = did it brilliantly; «sound» (Liverpool) = good, fine, right; «arvo» (Australia) = afternoon.
exercise: What is Painted Jaguar doing at the start of this part? | Sucking prickles out of his paws and saying his rhyme | Chasing the Hedgehog through the bushes and roaring | Asleep in his den | Fishing in the Amazon
exercise: «He'll never forget that this month of Sundays.» What does it mean? | He will not forget it for a very long time | He will forget it all by next Sunday morning | He only remembers things on Sundays | He has a calendar of Sundays
exercise: What does Hedgehog ask Tortoise to do first? | Hold up his chin so he can learn to swim | Unlace his prickles one by one, so he can bend | Teach him to climb trees | Hide him from Jaguar
exercise: What does Hedgehog do to help Tortoise curl? | Unlaces his back-plates a little | Rubs his shell all over with soft river-mud | Rolls him down the bank | Sings him a song
exercise: What does Tortoise manage after the first unlacing? | To curl up «a tiddy wee bit» | To roll up into a perfect ball at once | To climb a tree | To dive to the bottom
exercise: Why does Hedgehog tell Tortoise to stop curling for now? | It is making him black in the face | Jaguar is coming down the path | It is time for breakfast | The plates are falling off
exercise: Which swimming stroke does Hedgehog practise? | The side-stroke | The butterfly stroke | The breast-stroke | The doggy-paddle
exercise: Tortoise says that with more practice Hedgehog will be «a regular» what? | Whale | A big fish | Crocodile | A bull-frog
exercise: How many more holes does Tortoise ask Hedgehog to unlace? | Two | Only one | Three | Seven
exercise: Which exercise does Tortoise call «so peculiarly comfortable»? | Putting his hind legs round his ears | Standing on his head in the mud of the river bottom | Rolling down a hill | Hanging from a branch by his tail
exercise: What do the two friends keep saying while they practise? | «Won't Painted Jaguar be surprised!» | «We must hurry, it will soon be morning!» | «Humph!» | «Kun? Payah kun!»
exercise: What happens to Tortoise's back-plates? | They overlap, instead of lying side by side | They fall off in the night, one by one | They turn bright green and shiny, like wet leaves in the sun | They grow spikes
exercise: What happens to Hedgehog's prickles? | They melt into one another, like a pine-cone | They all fall out, one by one, into the turbid Amazon | They grow much longer and sharper | They turn into fur
exercise: «Less like a chestnut-burr.» What is a chestnut-burr? | The prickly case round a chestnut | A small brown bird that sings in chestnut trees | A burnt chestnut | A kind of beetle
exercise: What does Jaguar do when he sees the two new animals? | Falls backward over his tail three times | Runs straight home without a word to anyone | Laughs out loud | Bows politely
exercise: Which way do the friends say the rhyme to Jaguar? | Swapped: «Can't curl, but can swim, Stickly-Prickly, that's him!» | Exactly as Jaguar said it the night before, with nothing changed | Backwards, word by word | In French
exercise: What do the friends do after the rhyme? | Curl up and roll round and round Jaguar | Swim away down the Amazon | Climb up a palm-tree and laugh at him from the very top | Walk off politely
exercise: Jaguar says the two «have gone shares in their prickles». What does he mean? | They seem to have shared them out, so both are scaly | They have sold their prickles in the market for lettuces | They have lost all their prickles | They have hidden their prickles
exercise: What does Mother Jaguar say she would call the new animal? | «Armadillo», till she found the real name | «Hedgehog-and-Tortoise-together», till she was sure | «Painted Prickly» | «Pine-cone»
exercise: What else does Mother Jaguar advise? | Leave it alone | Eat it at once, while it is still sleepy from all the night's exercise | Drop it in the water | Ask the Baboon what to do
exercise: In the end-song, where does Kipling dream of going? | Down to Rio, in Brazil | Up the great grey-green Limpopo | To Fifty North and Forty West | To the shores of the Red Sea
exercise: From which English port do the steamers go «weekly», in the song? | Southampton | Liverpool, by the river Mersey | London, by the Thames | Bristol, in the west
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, why did Hedgehog decide to learn to swim? | Jaguar's rhyme said he «can't swim», so he would be caught in the water | He wanted to win a race against the fishes | The Tortoise dared him | He was too hot
exercise: Looking back: what did Mother Jaguar tell her son to do with a Tortoise? | Scoop him out of his shell with his paw | Drop him in the water so that he uncoils | Leave him alone | Roll him over
test: Mo: «they got their armour from a magic spell, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | No spell: they practised all night, helping each other, and changed | Right, Mother Jaguar cast it with her waving tail to stop them being eaten | A Djinn gave it to them | They found it at the bottom of the river
test: Why do Hedgehog and Tortoise each want the OTHER's skill? | Jaguar's rhyme tells him how to catch each one, so each needs what he lacks | They are bored with being themselves and want a change of shape for the fun of it | Their mothers told them to | They want to win a prize
test: What would happen now if Jaguar dropped one of them in the water? | It would swim away, since both can swim | It would uncoil and be easy to catch | It would sink like a stone to the muddy bottom of the river | It would turn back into a Hedgehog
test: «Excellent!» says each friend to the other again and again. What does this show? | They encourage each other kindly as they learn | They are making fun of each other's poor tries | They are tired and want to stop | They are shouting for Jaguar
test: Real life: what are real armadillos? | Mammals with bony plates covered in tough skin | A cross between a hedgehog and a tortoise that first lived by the Amazon | Reptiles like tortoises, laying eggs | Fish that walk on land
test: Real life: who gave armadillos their name? | Spanish speakers: «little armoured one» | Mother Jaguar, by the turbid Amazon | Kipling himself, in his book of 1902 | The ancient Greeks
test: Lily from Liverpool: «all armadillos roll up in a ball, sound?» (sound = right, OK) Put it right. | Only three-banded armadillos roll into a full ball | Yes, every single kind of armadillo can curl into a perfect ball, even the largest | None of them can bend at all | Only baby ones roll up
test: Grace from Sydney: «nine-banded armadillo went for a swim this arvo.» (arvo = afternoon) Could that be true? | Yes: nine-banded armadillos can swim and even walk along the bottom | No: armadillos sink at once and hate water, so they never go near rivers at all | No: armadillos only live in the sea | No: they only swim at night
test: Which is TRUE about this part of the story? | Jaguar falls backward over his own tail three times | Mother Jaguar knows the animal's real name | The friends eat Jaguar's breakfast while he is looking for his Mother | Hedgehog teaches Jaguar to swim
test: Looking back: which earlier animal also got a new shape by running and hopping all day, as these two did by practising all night? | Old Man Kangaroo, chased by Yellow-Dog Dingo | The Camel, who refused to work and was given a hump by the Djinn | The Whale | The Rhinoceros
test: Looking back: how did the Rhinoceros get the folds in his skin? | He rubbed against a palm-tree because of cake-crumbs inside his skin | He did exercises all night by the river with a friend | A Djinn magicked them | He was chased by a dog
test: Looking back: which friend in part 1 said «you can scoop me out of my shell if you like», though he had no shell? | Stickly-Prickly Hedgehog | Slow-Solid Tortoise, his friend | Painted Jaguar, to trick his Mother | Mother Jaguar, in her den
test: Looking back: what did Jaguar get in his paw the night before? | Prickles | A thorn from the fallen tree | A snail shell | Sticky mud
test: Ava: «Kipling ate with that ending, he'd been to Rio loads.» (ate = did it brilliantly) What does the song say? | He had never sailed the Amazon or reached Brazil, but would love to go | He had sailed to Rio every week from Southampton | He lived in Brazil as a boy | He saw a jaguar at the zoo in Rio
test: ★★★ Careful: when the friends say «Can't curl, but can swim — Stickly-Prickly, that's him!», why is this a trick on Jaguar? | Now BOTH can curl and BOTH can swim, so the rhyme no longer tells them apart | Stickly-Prickly still cannot swim at all, so the rhyme is simply a lie | It is a magic spell | Jaguar cannot hear it
test: ★★★ The Hedgehog had his back-plates... no, wait. Who had back-plates unlaced, and how many times does the story say they were unlaced? | Tortoise, twice: «a little», then «two holes more» | Hedgehog, once, by Tortoise | Tortoise, three times, by Jaguar | Hedgehog, twice, by his mother
test: ★★★ A friend says: «armadillos look like hedgehog plus tortoise, so they must come from them.» What is wrong with that thinking? | Looking alike does not prove two animals are related; armadillos are a separate family of mammals | Nothing is wrong, since armadillos really did come from the turbid Amazon exactly like that | Armadillos are birds | Hedgehogs come from armadillos instead
exam: Identify the means by which the Hedgehog and the Tortoise acquired each other's abilities, according to the narrative. | By mutual practice through the night, each assisting the other | By the intervention of a Djinn of the forest, summoned by Mother Jaguar at the request of her son | By consuming a magic lettuce | By the instruction of Painted Jaguar
exam: Ascertain the name proposed by Mother Jaguar for the creature that was «a little bit of both». | Armadillo | Pine-cone | Hedgehog | Tortoise
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the armour of real armadillos? | It consists of small bony plates covered with tough skin | It consists of hedgehog prickles that have melted together through prolonged soaking in a river | It consists of feathers | It consists of tortoise-shell borrowed in youth
exam: It is given that the end-song expresses a wish of its author. Select the response which most accurately characterises that wish. | To roll down to Rio some day before he is old | To return to Southampton | To capture a jaguar for the Zoological Gardens | To remain at home in perpetuity
